# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CLUE BANK MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED.
# These files contain every question and answer. They are generated locally by
# the scripts in tools/ and loaded straight into D1 with wrangler. If they ever
# reach GitHub, the whole point of moving the bank server-side is lost — and
# git history keeps them even after deletion.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/clues-production.sql
data/puzzles-production.sql
data/*-production.sql
data/ws-production.sql
# THE PATTERN ABOVE NEEDS THE SUFFIX, AND TWO IMPORTERS NO LONGER USE IT.
# `data/*-production.sql` does not match `data/wa-production.rewritten.sql`,
# which is exactly what Who Am I's and QuickFire's importers write when
# --rewrite-history is passed — a whole bank, answers included, under a name
# git was happy to track. Added 17 Sep 2026 after a review found it; no such
# file had been created yet, so nothing was committed and nothing leaked.
# The repository is PUBLIC. A bank that reaches GitHub is a bank that stays
# there, so these patterns are deliberately wider than the files that exist.
data/*-production*.sql
data/*.rewritten.sql
# AND THE SAME FILES WRITTEN FROM THE WRONG DIRECTORY. import_wordsearch.js
# and import_quickfire.js resolve their output against the CURRENT WORKING
# DIRECTORY rather than the repo root, so running either from tools/ writes
# tools/data/…-production.sql, which the anchored patterns above do not cover.
# The importers should be fixed to anchor on ROOT; until then this catches it.
**/data/*-production*.sql
**/data/*.rewritten.sql
# AND THE SAME FILES WITH SOMETHING AFTER THE .sql. Every pattern above needs
# the name to END in .sql, so a backup taken beside one is not covered: on
# 20 Sep 2026 data/sc-production.sql.before-20260920 — eight megabytes of the
# Scrambled bank — sat untracked and stageable in the tree, put there by a
# session taking a safety copy before an import. Nothing committed it, and
# only because staging is done by name here; one `git add -A` would have.
# The suffix is the whole fault: the file was the bank, named like the bank,
# and invisible to rules that all end in .sql.
data/*-production*.sql.*
**/data/*-production*.sql.*
# A CAPTURE IS STILL NOT COVERED BY ANY OF THIS, and cannot usefully be — a
# read-only JSON export of served boards matches none of these shapes and
# naming one to match would be guesswork about the next tool. The defence for
# those is where they are written, not what they are called: captures belong
# OUTSIDE the repository, beside it in ..\<game>xi-source\, which is where
# the Ballpark one of 20 Sep went.
# Grid XI's boards. The grid starts EMPTY, so every letter in one is an
# answer and a board committed here is a board solved. D1 is the only copy.
data/gd-production.sql
# The themed build plan: every board with its answer list. It exists to be read
# before import and must never be committed, for the same reason the generated
# SQL must not be — it is the clue bank with the answers attached.
data/themes-plan.json
data/themes-review.html
data/themes-review.csv
# Bespoke clue material, converted from the validated spreadsheet. Clues and
# answers, so it travels with the generated SQL rather than the repository.
data/bolton.json
data/other14.json

# Preview builds inline the answers so they can run offline — the exact thing
# moving the bank to D1 removed. Never commit one.
crosswordxi-preview-*.html
*.local.json

# Cloudflare / wrangler
.wrangler/
.dev.vars
wrangler.toml.local

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# OS and editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
node_modules/
.idea/
.vscode/

# render_test.mjs --SHOTS=1 writes a PNG per viewport here. Useful to look at,
# not useful to keep: sixteen screenshots of a build that has moved on.
shots/

# The local tester is generated and is not part of the site.
scrambled-tester.html
